"concept":{"x":6120,"y":0,"w":640,"page":"mihrab","fill":"blue","text":"Concept — Mihrab (محراب)\n\nA mihrab is the niche that shows a room which way to face for prayer. On Afnon it does the same job for the screen: one tall arch that always holds the next prayer and its countdown, readable from the sofa without picking up the remote. Everything else — apps, settings, Wi-Fi — lives in the open courtyard beside it.\n\nThe arch is a sanctuary: the Qur'anic verse about prayer at its fixed times sits in its crown and nothing — no tile, toast or focus ring — ever enters it. The arch changes what it says (Asr, Jumu'ah, Iftar, the call itself) but never where it is, so the daily rhythm becomes a single habit of glance.\n\nFlat lapis, gold and plaster need no blur or video; one static star lattice gives texture for free. The screensaver dims to a drifting clock and next-prayer line on true black, so an LED panel never holds a bright static shape."}
